ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/plan.md:303:  - **Batch-size option (corrected 2026-09-29):** `winner_ce_6m` is not an Isaiah-aligned fallback. Relative to `scaling_6m` it changes `value_loss` to `winner_ce`, requires `env.reward_mode: win_only` and `value_mode: win_only`, and uses warmup 200 / decay 150k / `lr_min_ratio` 0.02 and muon weight decay 0.05 (`configs/winner_ce_6m.yaml`); our economic reward is not a winner distribution, and the merged 3.2/3.4 guards reject it. (c1) The full `winner_ce_6m` recipe is a reward-design change: owner decision. (c2) Its batch shape alone (512 envs × horizon 128, spm 32; 64 envs/rank, spm 4 at 8 ranks) on `scaling_6m` settings is a recipe deviation (4× the transitions per optimizer step): if 32 envs/rank underutilizes the GPUs (low GPU busy share; rollout or train-step time dominated by launch overhead), it needs a measured comparison against the divided `scaling_6m` shape (complete-work SPS and learning at equal env steps) before adoption, recorded in the recipe Decision. See the multi-GPU Decision.
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/plan.md:325:- **Owner directives covered:** clean base and reference branch (C1–C17, dispositions); same layer topology (I0b, 2.1–2.3); extra BC (Phase 5); 2/4-rank RTX PRO 6000 (Phase 6) and the likely 8-rank main run (6.3b, owner approval before the pod); Codex (Working with Codex, streams); Isaiah's principles and stateless model (I0–I12).
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:47:- `_evaluation_scores_and_metrics` decides each evaluation game. A Kaggriculture config (`cfg.env.obs_spec` is `KaggricultureObsConfig`) uses the raw banks. Orbit returns Isaiah's accumulated returns unchanged.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:54:- `_truncation_keeps_transition_reward(obs)` sets the reward rule: `ObsBatch` (Orbit) returns `False`, so Isaiah's zeroing stays; `KaggricultureObsBatch` returns `True`; any other type raises `TypeError`.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:62:- These are `configs/scaling_6m.yaml`'s settings, so the guards encode Isaiah's recipe; `vf_clip_coef` stays free.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:71:  - `_create_eval_env` builds Isaiah's unseeded Orbit `VectorizedEnv` with the same arguments as before.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:73:- Isaiah's evaluation count is kept: `cfg.env.n_envs` games on the main process, with no `eval_n_games` knob.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:74:- Every evaluation log now carries `eval/games`, `eval/promoted` (1 or 0) and `eval/promotion_threshold` (0.7). The evaluation record is logged once, after the incumbent refresh, teacher update, promoted-checkpoint write and barrier complete (the reference's order), so a failed promotion leaves no `eval/promoted` record. Isaiah logged the evaluation before promoting; this moves that log, and a promotion failure now loses that step's evaluation metrics along with the run.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:93:- **L2:** `make_obs` contract batches keep a 0.02/−0.02 economic reward on a cut row, and the GAE return there equals that reward plus the bootstrap. Orbit zeroes it. A `PPOTrainer` rollout on a truncating tiny Orbit env pins Isaiah's behavior end to end: the cut step has zero reward, done, a truncated flag and a bootstrap equal to `compute_value` of the cut state.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:104:- A validated Kaggriculture env now runs Task 3.4's `_validate_kaggriculture_rl` (gamma 1, no `winner_ce`) and then `_validate_kaggriculture_training` above (adds `rl.value_loss: mse` and `rl.ppo_clip_mode: per_player`; `env.reward_mode` is also fixed to `win_loss` by the env schema). Orbit configs run Isaiah's checks in `_validate_orbit_constraints`. The earlier `isinstance(env.obs_spec, KaggricultureObsConfig)` branch on Orbit's `EnvConfig` was unreachable for validated configs and was dropped.
cookbook/references/evaluation-and-truncation-follow-the-kaggriculture-objective.md:113:- **Orbit keeps a quirk.** Its truncation zeroes the whole cut row, including a 4-player elimination reward (−1) earned on that same step. That is Isaiah's behavior, recorded here, not changed.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:4:description: "Qualify on two RTX 5090/RTX PRO 6000 GPUs before four; keep the pod running after bounded runs at the owner’s request; host history lives in ops. Owner 2026-09-29: eight RTX PRO 6000 likely for the main run, two ranks for diagnosis and building; the Isaiah-divided 8-rank config (32 envs/rank, spm 2) is CPU-tested and its 8-GPU qualification (Task 6.3b, owner-approved pod) is open. Isaiah's only 8-GPU config (scaling_50m) keeps 256 envs/rank; his 6M multi-GPU precedent divides. winner_ce_6m is not an Isaiah-aligned fallback: its full recipe is an owner reward-design decision and its batch shape alone a measured recipe deviation."
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:9:sources: [{"resource": "user-directive:2026-09-29:fit-rtx6000-resources"}, {"resource": "user-directive:2026-09-29:extra-bc-rtx6000-codex"}, {"resource": "reference-branch:kg/reference-2026-09-29/ops/cookbook-cleanup-2026-09-29/host-history.md"}, {"resource": "reference-branch:kg/reference-2026-09-29/ops/default4096-2026-09-29/plan.md"}, {"resource": "reference-branch:kg/reference-2026-09-29/ops/default4096-2026-09-29/host-replacement.json"}, {"resource": "reference-branch:kg/reference-2026-09-29/ops/gpu-sps-2026-09-29/results.md"}, {"resource": "user-directive:2026-09-28:four-rank-first"}, {"resource": "user-directive:2026-09-28:two-gpu-sps-first"}, {"resource": "repository:ops/cookbook-setup-checks.md"}, {"resource": "user-directive:2026-09-29:eight-rank-main-run-two-rank-diagnostics"}, {"resource": "repository:configs/kaggriculture_8rank.yaml"}, {"resource": "repository:tests/kaggriculture/test_configs.py"}, {"resource": "repository:tests/scripts/test_run_ppo.py"}, {"resource": "repository:ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/plan.md"}, {"resource": "repository:ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/8rank/py-prepare.log"}, {"resource": "repository:ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/8rank/mutation-spm4.log"}, {"resource": "repository:configs/scaling_6m.yaml"}, {"resource": "repository:configs/scaling_50m.yaml"}, {"resource": "repository:configs/winner_ce_6m.yaml"}, {"resource": "repository:configs/winner_ce_6m_4x5090.yaml"}, {"resource": "repository:configs/model/stateless_transformer_6m_win_only.yaml"}, {"resource": "repository:python/owl/train/config.py"}, {"resource": "pending-merge-branch:kg/rebuild-gpu-checks/ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/results.md"}]
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:18:The owner later preferred **two RTX 5090 or two RTX PRO 6000 GPUs first** for qualification; that supersedes the four-rank-first preference. Four and eight ranks are later scales. This sets a resource direction, not a demonstrated scaling benefit or optimal recipe. Under Isaiah's multi-GPU rule the global configuration stays identical across rank counts (see the [[recipe-choices-align-to-isaiah-without-owner-escalation|alignment Decision]]); qualify correctness, memory and complete-update throughput on two ranks before scaling to four.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:28:## Fit Isaiah's recipe to our GPUs — owner, 2026-09-29
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:30:> when fitting in GPUs, make sure we fits the GPU resource, since we are doing on RTX 6000 while Isaiah is doing in Tufa's B200 clusters
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:34:Sources: `user-directive:2026-09-29:fit-rtx6000-resources`, `user-directive:2026-09-29:stay-on-torch-2-9`. Keep Isaiah's global configuration and fit only the per-rank shapes and the spm/accumulation split, as his own `winner_ce_6m_4x5090` does for 32 GB RTX 5090s. The target peak is ≤ 85% of 97,887 MiB per rank, measured at the densest states. Throughput is measured, not assumed from B200 results. Torch stays at Isaiah's pinned 2.9.0; the compiler overflow is handled by the batch cadence, trunk chunking and a first-minibatch log-ratio alarm ([[../references/compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows|compiler overflow Reference]]). The resource-fit rules are in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/plan.md`.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:36:## Eight ranks likely for the main run — owner, 2026-09-29
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:38:> we'd likely use 8Xrtx 6000 in main run, 2 rank for diagonose/building.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:40:Source: `user-directive:2026-09-29:eight-rank-main-run-two-rank-diagnostics`. The owner said "likely"; this records a stated expectation, **not** a final adoption of eight ranks, of an 8-GPU pod or of any run.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:42:- **Roles.** The current two-rank RTX PRO 6000 pod stays the host for smoke, diagnostics and building (plan Tasks 6.1–6.2). Eight RTX PRO 6000 GPUs are the likely main-run hardware, qualified in plan Task 6.3b. The four-rank step (6.3) stays optional between them.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:43:- **Shape.** `configs/kaggriculture_8rank.yaml` applies Isaiah's 6M multi-GPU division (`winner_ce_6m_4x5090.yaml`; see the precedents below): 32 envs/rank, `segments_per_minibatch` 2, accumulation 1, so the global batch (256 envs), optimizer steps per iteration (16), global segments per step (16) and transitions per iteration (16,384) equal `scaling_6m`. The optimizer, schedule, PPO coefficients, compile settings and teacher settings are unchanged. `teacher_segments_per_minibatch` stays 128 as in Isaiah's per-rank configs; it is a precompute chunk size, so each rank precomputes its 32 envs in one chunk.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:44:- **Checks on this version (CPU only).** `tests/kaggriculture/test_configs.py` derives every per-rank shape from `scaling_6m` divided by the world size and fails loudly when a global quantity, a per-rank minibatch or a teacher chunk is not a whole division (world sizes 3 and 32, accumulation 3 and teacher chunk 24 are rejected). It asserts the 8-rank global workload equals `scaling_6m`, and that the startup GEMM check sees 64 rollout, 256 minibatch, 4,096 teacher-chunk and 64 evaluation rows, one trunk and one head call each. `tests/scripts/test_run_ppo.py` drives `run_ppo.main` from the 8-rank YAML to the headroom lines and the explicit not-wired stop. Changing the YAML to spm 4 turned four config tests red and restoring it byte-for-byte turned them green (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/8rank/mutation-spm4.log`). `OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 uvx --from rust-just just py-prepare` passed: ruff, format, mypy (62 files), 1,534 passed and 5 skipped (native grammar binding, two FlashAttention/CUDA, quantized backend, native Kaggriculture env), docs-fresh (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/8rank/py-prepare.log`). No GPU, Rust or training check ran.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:45:- **Component estimate, not end-to-end.** The GPU checks bundle ("GPU checks bundle (component)" in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/results.md` on branch `kg/rebuild-gpu-checks`, ops evidence pending merge into this branch) timed the model alone at the 8-rank per-rank shape: 1.389 s (mid) and 2.372 s (dense) per update, or 11,796 and 6,906 global env steps/s if ranks scaled perfectly. That is 0.90 (mid) and 0.95 (dense) of four times the 2-rank rate; the loss is mostly the smaller train step. It excludes the engine, host copies, GAE, logging and all-reduce, and it ran on 2 GPUs of a 2-GPU pod with synthetic observations and fresh weights. It predicts no end-to-end SPS.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:46:- **Open measurements (Task 6.3b).** The 8-GPU pod is a new billable resource: read and state its live price and get the owner's approval before creating it. Then measure, at 8-rank shapes: dense-state memory against the 85% target; complete-work SPS in env steps and learner turns, with phase costs; gradient all-reduce cost on the pod's actual interconnect (RTX PRO 6000 has no NVLink; read the topology rather than assume PCIe behavior); vCPUs per GPU against the Rust engine's step time and `native_threads`; the first-minibatch log-ratio alarm on every rank; W&B live sync; and seed-stream disjointness at world size 8 (the Task 1.4 L3 test includes world size 8).
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:47:- **Isaiah's multi-GPU precedents.** His only 8-GPU config is `configs/scaling_50m.yaml` (50M model, 8× B200, 4B env steps): 256 envs/rank, spm 8 × accumulation 2, horizon 64, so its global batch (2,048 envs) grows with the rank count, as `scaling_12m` (2× B200) and `scaling_25m` (4× B200) also keep 256 envs/rank. His 6M multi-GPU precedent, `configs/winner_ce_6m_4x5090.yaml`, instead divides `winner_ce_6m`'s `n_envs` and `segments_per_minibatch` by 4 and keeps its global batch. `kaggriculture_8rank.yaml` follows the 6M division because the goal is `scaling_6m`'s global workload at every rank count; there is no Isaiah 8-GPU 6M config, and `scaling_50m`'s growth comes with a larger model and budget, so it does not license enlarging a 6M batch.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:48:- **Batch-size option (corrected 2026-09-29).** `winner_ce_6m` is **not** an Isaiah-aligned fallback for Kaggriculture. Relative to `scaling_6m`, `configs/winner_ce_6m.yaml` changes `rl.value_loss` to `winner_ce`, requires `env.reward_mode: win_only` and `value_mode: win_only` (model `stateless_transformer_6m_win_only`), uses a different LR schedule (warmup 200, decay 150,000, `lr_min_ratio` 0.02) and muon weight decay 0.05, and also sets `max_entities` 256, `two_player_weight` 0.5 and teacher chunk 256; its header calls it a value-loss ablation of `baseline.yaml`. Our economic reward is not a winner distribution, and the merged Task 3.2/3.4 guards in `python/owl/train/config.py` reject `winner_ce` and any reward mode other than `win_loss`. Two variants are recorded; neither is adopted:
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:49:  - **(c1) Full `winner_ce_6m` recipe:** a reward-design change (win-only reward, winner cross-entropy critic, its own schedule). It needs an owner decision and would reopen the reward and critic semantics, not just the config.
cookbook/decisions/start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks.md:50:  - **(c2) Batch shape only:** 512 envs × horizon 128, spm 32 (64 envs/rank, spm 4 at 8 ranks) on `scaling_6m` settings. This matches neither Isaiah recipe: each of the 16 optimizer steps per iteration sees 4,096 transitions instead of 1,024 (65,536 per iteration instead of 16,384) under `scaling_6m`'s step-defined schedule. It is a recipe deviation, so adopting it requires a measured comparison against the divided `scaling_6m` shape (complete-work throughput and learning at equal env steps), recorded in the [[recipe-choices-align-to-isaiah-without-owner-escalation|alignment Decision]]. Low GPU utilization at 32 envs/rank is the trigger to measure, not a reason to switch.
cookbook/decisions/index.md:3:- [[restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base|Restart the port from Isaiah's clean base]] — Restart from Isaiah's 32b3ec9 with reviewed reference reuse and the same layer topology; BC and RTX PRO 6000 verification follow. Task 1.1 rebuilds a standalone hash-pinned kernel: 59 engine tests, four replays/2,876 transitions, green preparation (769 Python cases), isolated Cargo checks and explicit vendored-style exceptions; checker hardened after verification round 1.
cookbook/decisions/index.md:4:- [[v3-reuses-isaiah-infrastructure-with-kaggriculture-semantics|V3 reuses Isaiah infrastructure with Kaggriculture semantics]] — Deliver one complete final adaptation of the efficient Isaiah pipeline that respects Isaiah's own principles in their game form; partial mechanisms are not finished deliverables, and the rejected decoder remains historical.
cookbook/decisions/index.md:6:- [[the-policy-is-stateless-and-observation-only|The policy is stateless and observation-only]] — Carry forward explicit current-observation tokens and the prohibition on opponent identity conditioning; Isaiah's StatelessTransformerV1, never his recurrent model, is the reference contract, with the same layer topology and only game I/O differing.
cookbook/decisions/index.md:9:- [[start-multi-gpu-qualification-with-two-ranks|Start multi-GPU qualification with two ranks]] — Qualify on two GPUs before four; 2/4-rank RTX PRO 6000 verification is authorized; keep the pod running after bounded runs; host history lives in ops. Owner 2026-09-29: eight RTX PRO 6000 likely for the main run, two ranks for diagnosis and building; the Isaiah-divided 8-rank config (32 envs/rank, spm 2) is CPU-tested, and its qualification (Task 6.3b) awaits an owner-approved 8-GPU pod. Isaiah's only 8-GPU config (scaling_50m) keeps 256 envs/rank while his 6M precedent divides; winner_ce_6m is not an aligned fallback (full recipe: owner reward decision; batch shape alone: measured deviation).
cookbook/decisions/index.md:10:- [[preserve-the-ppo-recipe-across-bc-bootstrap|Preserve the PPO recipe across BC bootstrap]] — The BC-period two-rank 4096-env fixed-minibatch recipe is preserved as a historical snapshot with separate checkpoint custody; later runs follow Isaiah alignment and teacher-adapter completion.
cookbook/decisions/index.md:11:- [[recipe-choices-align-to-isaiah-without-owner-escalation|Recipe choices align to Isaiah without owner escalation]] — Optimizer-step cadence and critic/value-distillation semantics are implementer choices resolved toward Isaiah scaling_6m and his 6M multi-GPU division, not owner decisions; both are encoded in the rebuilt configs and model and pass CPU config/startup checks, while trainer integration and GPU qualification remain pending.
cookbook/decisions/index.md:12:- [[kaggriculture-compiles-gemms-with-cublas-only|Kaggriculture compiles GEMMs with cuBLAS only]] — For the owner's request that the compiled-GEMM crash never recur, compiled Kaggriculture regions use Inductor's `"ATEN"` (cuBLAS) GEMM backends instead of Isaiah's default, claimed at every public compile entry behind a probed torch 2.9.0 / triton 3.5.0 / driver 595.91.07 check; implementer's measured choice (+4.5–6.5 % model-only update wall), CPU-tested enforcement, guards kept, Orbit unchanged.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:3:title: "Kaggriculture encoder reuses Isaiah's stateless layers"
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:4:description: "The rebuilt encoder uses Isaiah's StatelessTransformerV1 classes, token roles, packing, compile hook and initialization; only stem input widths are game-specific, and guards chunk both the padded and packed compiled GEMMs below 2^31."
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:11:# Kaggriculture encoder reuses Isaiah's stateless layers
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:16:- **`python/owl/model/base.py`:** `BaseModelAPI[ObsT, ActT, ActSpecT]` and `ModelOutput` / `ModelServingOutput[ActT]` use PEP 696 defaults, so Isaiah's bare annotations keep meaning Orbit. `count_non_masked_tokens` narrows to `ObsBatch` with `isinstance` and requires other games to override it. `python/owl/train/optimizer.py` accepts any game's model.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:21:  - Isaiah's flash packing, compile hook and initialization helpers, reused unchanged
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:25:- **`configs/model/kaggriculture.yaml`:** Isaiah's 6m ladder point with depth 8. Encoder: 5,312,768 parameters.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:26:- **`tests/owl/model/test_model_config_files.py`:** loads the new preset; since Task 3.1 through the shared loader, like Isaiah's presets.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:33:  - topology against Isaiah's 6m classes; initialization (residual, hidden √2 and input gains by singular values, token std ≈ `D^-0.5`, LayerNorm 1/0)
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:46:Task 2.2 adds Isaiah's critic: an `OutputProjectionMLP` over the two critic-value tokens, a winner softmax (masked as Isaiah's since Task 3.1), value `2p(self) − 1`, output gain 1.0 excluded from Muon. Task 2.3's action heads and the full-model budget (6,252,223 parameters) are in the [[kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection|heads Reference]]. Shared factory registration, the trunk compile dispatch and the masked critic are in the [[kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic|Task 3.1 model-side Reference]]; trainer integration is open. `check_contract` checks dtypes, shapes and bounds, not the semantic zero-fill and privacy rules; those are asserted only for the synthetic fixture, and the native writer (Task 1.x) must be tested against them separately.
cookbook/references/bc-bootstrap-uses-native-replay-features-and-current-heads.md:33:BC quality remains unqualified: the fixed held-out subset NLL reaches0.761 at14,600 updates, then degrades to1.954 at75,600 while training loss falls. This is overfitting on the selected corpus; longer runtime alone does not establish improvement. Best weights are retained. Full held-out evaluation, action-level fidelity and free-running economic behavior remain unverified. The owner subsequently requests a separate half-hour PPO from BC best; BC is stopped early for that switch, so there is no completed75-minute BC result. The separate launch is documented in `ops/ppo-from-bc-2026-09-29/plan.md`. It failed: after 3 iterations and only 6 optimizer steps (KL 1.8894/1.0453/0.3651; win rates 12.5/10.55/3.52% against the BC incumbent), rollout hit a CUDA illegal memory access and rank 1 then hit the 600 s NCCL timeout. A later blocking reproduction traced it to a compiler-generated GEMM overflow on the 8,192-row rollout forward, which also corrupted rollout activations. The KL spikes and falling win rates are therefore not evidence against the BC policy ([[compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows|compiler overflow Reference]]). No final checkpoint exists; the BC best and its pre-PPO copy have matching SHA-256. Automatic handoff remains disabled. The planned Isaiah alignment changes the architecture (per-player critic value tokens, possibly model size), so it schedules one BC rerun (`ops/gap-closure-2026-09-29/plan.md`, Phase 4). Preserve the [[../decisions/preserve-the-ppo-recipe-across-bc-bootstrap|PPO recipe]]; do not infer readiness or promotion from these local-loss smoke checks.
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:3:title: "Restart the port from Isaiah's clean base"
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:4:description: "Restart from Isaiah's 32b3ec9 with the prior port as a reviewed reference; preserve layer topology, rerun BC and verify RTX PRO 6000. Task 1.1 rebuilds the isolated, hash-pinned rules kernel with 59 passing engine tests and four native-reset replays; explicit tooling exceptions preserve vendored bytes."
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:12:# Restart the port from Isaiah's clean base
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:26:- Local `main` and `kg/isaiah-gap-closure` were reset to Isaiah's `32b3ec9`. The remote was not changed: `origin/main` is still `0d01234`, and nothing was pushed.
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:33:- **References describe the reference branch, not the current tree.** Sources whose files no longer exist are re-pointed as `reference-branch:kg/reference-2026-09-29/<path>`. Sources for files that still exist (Isaiah's own files) stay `repository:` so the first-edit gate still surfaces them. Until the rebuild, those notes describe the reference branch's adapted versions of those files.
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:35:- **Same layer topology:** the rebuilt model uses Isaiah's `StatelessTransformerV1` layer classes, arrangement and roles: observation stems, learned per-role tokens, trunk blocks, critic head and actor input projection. Only the game I/O differs: input channel widths, categorical encodings fed to the stems, and the action heads. See the [[the-policy-is-stateless-and-observation-only|stateless Decision]].
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:126:> Found that Isaiah's lockfile pins kaggle-environments 1.29.0, which lacks Kaggriculture, while 1.32.7 (already cached locally) has it. this we will have to update it.
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:128:Source: `user-directive:2026-09-29:update-kaggle-environments-1-32-7`. Isaiah's project took `kaggle-environments` from git HEAD, locked at 1.29.0, which has no Kaggriculture environment. It is now pinned to `==1.32.7` via `uv remove` + `uv add`, which also removes the git source override. 1.32.7's `envs/kaggriculture/kaggriculture.py` has SHA-256 `bc8a5487…`, the engine the vendored Rust kernel targets.
cookbook/decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base.md:130:Isaiah's critical pins are unchanged: torch 2.9.0, triton 3.5.0, flash-attn 2.8.3, numpy, pydantic, wandb, mypy. The other 55 changed dependency names (plus the `owl` requirement record) are kaggle-environments' own dependency tree (e.g. jax 0.10 → 0.11.2; `click` is shared with W&B, whose live telemetry was not exercised). Checks with 1.32.7: `just prepare` passes, with Rust 155 passed / 2 ignored (expensive action-angle audits; Orbit replay and generation parity pass) and Python 722 passed / 3 skipped. Codex independently reproduced the lock byte-for-byte from the recorded `uv remove` + `uv add` (`ops/deps-independent-verification-fb65e1f/`).
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:3:title: "Kaggriculture model joins Isaiah's factory, trunk compile and masked critic"
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:4:description: "Task 3.1 model side: KaggricultureTransformerConfig is in the shared ModelConfig union and create_model with game-checked specs, the trunk compile target dispatches through a nominal TrunkCompileAPI that compiles only the blocks behind _run_trunk's overflow guard, and the winner softmax uses Isaiah's masked form; CPU tests only; FullConfig now pairs it with the Task 3.4 KaggricultureEnvConfig."
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:11:# Kaggriculture model joins Isaiah's factory, trunk compile and masked critic
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:19:  - `python/owl/model/factory.py`: `create_model` keeps Isaiah's exhaustive `match` ending in `assert_never`, but matches on the config class instead of his `config.model_arch` strings (Isaiah `32b3ec9`), so each branch narrows to its game's config type. Each architecture belongs to one game. An Orbit model given Kaggriculture specs raises `TypeError`, and so does the reverse. Overloads give Orbit callers `BaseModelAPI` and Kaggriculture callers `KaggricultureTransformer`. A caller holding the full union gets `BaseModelAPI[Any, Any, Any]`.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:23:  - `configure_model_compile` accepts any `nn.Module`. For `trunk` it dispatches with `isinstance` (no `getattr`). The recurrent model keeps Isaiah's specific error, and any other model raises.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:26:- **Critic.** The Task 2.2 brief asked for a masked winner softmax, and Isaiah uses `masked_softmax(logits, still_playing)`. The code used a plain `log_softmax`.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:27:  - `_winner_log_probabilities` now follows Isaiah's `_critic_distillation`. It fills masked logits with the dtype minimum, then applies `log_softmax`.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:29:  - Live rows are unchanged. An all-masked row gets Isaiah's uniform masked result (value 0).
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:30:  - **Recorded deviation:** Isaiah's `_critic_logits` raises on a row with no live player; this model does not. The heads already emit empty programs for inactive rows, and contract v4 keeps `still_playing` true. The difference is listed in `docs/kaggriculture-model.md` (resolved toward Isaiah's output, not escalated).
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:51:  - with distinct non-zero critic tokens on every row, the probabilities equal Isaiah's `masked_softmax`, live rows equal the unmasked softmax, and finished rows are uniform with value 0
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:54:- Isaiah's suites, `pytest tests/owl tests/scripts tests/tools -m "not slow"`: 1,048 passed, 3 skipped (`isaiah-suites.log`). The plan's `--extra reference` flag no longer exists in this tree, so it was dropped. No Rust changed, so `cargo test` was not run.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:55:- Codex verification r1 (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/verify-3.1-rest-r1.md`, evidence in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/verify-3.1-rest/`): APPROVE WITH EDITS, no functional defect. It reran pytest (1,324 passed, 4 skipped), Isaiah's suites (1,048 passed, 3 skipped) and mypy, and two mutations (compile-guard bypass, unmasked critic) failed their intended tests. Its two wording findings are applied here.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:56:- Codex verification r2 (`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/verify-3.1-rest-r2.md`): APPROVE WITH EDITS, no functional defect or shim wrapper; both r1 findings resolved. It reran pytest (1,324 passed, 4 skipped), Isaiah's suites (1,048 passed, 3 skipped), mypy (57 files) and the same two mutations. Its one finding, a stale current-state claim in the [[compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows|compiled-GEMM Reference]], is fixed there.
cookbook/references/shared-ppo-adapts-game-batches-without-a-second-loop.md:4:description: "One shared Isaiah PPO trainer carries Kaggriculture batches; its core is upstream-equivalent, the recipe is partially aligned, and teacher, cadence, CUDA-fault and GPU-qualification gaps are tracked in ops."
cookbook/references/shared-ppo-adapts-game-batches-without-a-second-loop.md:15:`scripts/run_ppo.py` and `python/owl/train/ppo.py` remain the one trainer. Kaggriculture batches flow through Isaiah's collection, storage, GAE, minibatch, optimizer and incumbent lifecycle; no second PPO loop exists.
cookbook/references/shared-ppo-adapts-game-batches-without-a-second-loop.md:18:- The GPU YAMLs still run the historical 4096 env/rank, spm 1, accumulation 2 cadence: 2,048 optimizer steps per iteration against upstream's 16, with four-times-faster scheduler exposure per env step. The [[../decisions/recipe-choices-align-to-isaiah-without-owner-escalation|alignment Decision]] resolves it toward Isaiah's multi-GPU rule.
cookbook/references/shared-ppo-adapts-game-batches-without-a-second-loop.md:29:Against upstream `32b3ec9`, the GAE, attention and shared actor-projection modules are byte-identical. The PPO loss, update/minibatch/normalization, optimizer/scheduler and `TransformerBlock` bodies match after annotation normalization (`ops/isaiah-alignment-2026-09-29/infrastructure-audit.md`). No upstream file is deleted, and upstream tests are only extended. On 2026-09-29 the full suites pass on current source: 814 Python (3 hardware skips) and 155 Rust (2 ignored); Isaiah's Orbit tests are included. These are CPU/software checks, not GPU or learning qualification. Game-specific storage, model dispatch, evaluation adapters and config semantics are the integration surface.
cookbook/decisions/kaggriculture-compiles-gemms-with-cublas-only.md:4:description: "To keep the compiled-GEMM CUDA crash from recurring, every compiled Kaggriculture region runs with Inductor's max_autotune_gemm_backends set to \"ATEN\" (cuBLAS) instead of Isaiah's default \"ATEN,TRITON,CPP\", after a startup check rejects an unprobed torch, triton or NVIDIA driver. The owner asked for the outcome; the implementer chose the setting from a measured A/B (+4.5–6.5 % model-only update wall). Enforcement is CPU-tested only, and the overflow guards stay."
cookbook/decisions/kaggriculture-compiles-gemms-with-cublas-only.md:26:- **cuBLAS only.** Every compiled Kaggriculture region runs with `torch._inductor.config.max_autotune_gemm_backends = "ATEN"`, so compiled `mm`/`addmm`/`bmm` lower to extern cuBLAS calls. Isaiah's torch 2.9.0 default is `"ATEN,TRITON,CPP"`. Every public compile entry point applies the setting before `torch.compile`: `configure_model_compile` for the `mlp` target, and `KaggricultureTransformer.compile_transformer_trunk` itself for the `trunk` target, whether `configure_model_compile` or a caller invokes it directly. The claim lives in `python/owl/model/compile_gemm.py`.
cookbook/decisions/kaggriculture-compiles-gemms-with-cublas-only.md:36:- **Orbit unchanged, one game per process.** Isaiah's Orbit models keep the backends they find. The setting is process-global, so the first compile claims it for one game. `StatelessTransformerV1.compile_transformer_trunk` claims Orbit itself, like the configured paths. Compiling the other game in the same process raises, in either order and through any entry point, before any value changes. A lock serializes the claim's check and write, so this also holds when threads claim concurrently.
cookbook/decisions/kaggriculture-compiles-gemms-with-cublas-only.md:98:- **Orbit.** Isaiah's Orbit path keeps the vulnerable default. It is outside v3's Kaggriculture scope, and this change does not protect it.
cookbook/references/failed-training-reports-status-before-distributed-cleanup.md:23:This is rebuild Task 0.3 on Isaiah's clean base, serving the
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection.md:3:title: "Kaggriculture grammar heads sit behind Isaiah's actor projection"
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection.md:4:description: "Task 2.3 adds Isaiah's 3D→D actor input projection and batched grammar heads with exact coupled-Gumbel HIRE sampling, same-path replay with support/length/canonical flags and one host check, head GEMM-extent chunking, and a 6,252,223-parameter preset; synthetic grammar tables stand in until the Task 1.4 binding exposes the Task 1.2 native tables (which exist and match the stand-in bit-for-bit)."
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection.md:11:# Kaggriculture grammar heads sit behind Isaiah's actor projection
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection.md:15:- **Topology.** `actor_input_proj = nn.Linear(3D, D)` over `[entity ‖ self player ‖ plan]`, as Isaiah's `source_actor_input_proj`. The market queue has no entity, so it uses `[plan ‖ player ‖ plan]`. `KaggricultureGrammarActor` (`python/owl/model/kaggriculture_actor.py`) holds everything game-specific:
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection.md:32:  - Sampling adds no policy-validation host synchronization: `forward` never runs the replay check. The encode is not sync-free: packed trunk dispatch sizes its packing on the host (Isaiah's `build_packed_sequence`), and an oversized packed batch transfers per-row token counts to plan chunks. (Corrected after Codex's Task 2.3 verification, `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/codex/verify-2.3-r1.md`.)
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection.md:34:- **Initialization and optimizer.** The embedding `.weight`s are in `get_input_layers` (std `D^-0.5`). Every `head.out` is in `get_output_layers` with gain 0.01; `reset_parameters` now follows Isaiah's output-layer loop. `actor_input_proj` and every head `.up` go to Muon.
cookbook/decisions/throughput-means-correct-complete-work.md:24:Use [[../workflows/profile-cuda-bottlenecks-with-nsight-systems|Nsight Systems]] for NVIDIA timeline attribution. Temporary downscaling can diagnose a fault but does not close the throughput requirement. Measured scopes so far (the rejected old decoder and the historical fixed-minibatch sweep) live in `ops/gpu-sps-2026-09-29/results.md`. None of them establishes playing strength, dense-game capacity or an owner-specified performance floor, and each recipe change, including the Isaiah-aligned cadence, needs its own complete-work qualification.
cookbook/references/starter-history-and-knowledge-remain-retrievable.md:4:description: "The Isaiah source history/license, redirected remotes and selective cookbook lifecycle are retained with explicit verification limits."
cookbook/references/starter-history-and-knowledge-remain-retrievable.md:15:The root Git history starts from Isaiah's `32b3ec900ad406eedd965f53a1a0f4490d31c589` (`add slideshow`). `origin` is `git@github.com:theredbluepill/kg-v3.git`; `upstream` is `https://github.com/IsaiahPressman/kaggle-orbit-wars.git`. These were read directly with `git log -1` and `git remote -v`. Root `LICENSE` retains IsaiahPressman's MIT notice and matches the imported commit. Root README now explains v3 setup and adaptation boundaries; the upstream Orbit Wars README is retained at `docs/upstream-orbit-wars.md`. This task makes no commit/push claim.
